Test an authenticated request
If you haven't generated a Sanctum token yet, open Tinker:
php artisan tinker

Run:
$user = App\Models\User::find(1);

Check that the user exists:
$user;

Generate a token:
$token = $user->createToken('postman');

Display the plain-text token:
$token->plainTextToken;

| Concept | What your test verifies |
|---|---|
| Feature testing | Tests the API through Laravel's HTTP request lifecycle. |
| `RefreshDatabase` | Prepares a clean test database state. |
| `Sanctum::actingAs()` | Authenticates a user for the API request. |
| `postJson()` | Sends a JSON POST request to your API. |
| `assertCreated()` | Verifies HTTP `201 Created`. |
| `assertDatabaseHas()` | Verifies that employee data was saved. |
| `assertUnprocessable()` | Verifies HTTP `422` for invalid input. |
| `assertJsonValidationErrors()` | Checks which fields failed validation. |
| `assertUnauthorized()` | Verifies HTTP `401` for unauthenticated requests. |


actingAs   
makes that user the authenticated user for subsequent requests in the test.Sanctum::actingAs() is a testing helper. It does not test the complete process of generating and submitting a real API token. That requires a separate authentication test.

use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\RefreshDatabase;
Its main purpose is to prepare the database for tests and manage database changes so tests can run in isolation. 

it() is a Pest function that defines a test.

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `$this->get()` | Simulate an HTTP GET request |
| `$this->getJson()` | Simulate a GET request expecting JSON |
| `$this->post()` | Simulate an HTTP POST request |
| `$this->postJson()` | Simulate a JSON POST request |
| `$this->putJson()` | Simulate a JSON PUT request |
| `$this->patchJson()` | Simulate a JSON PATCH request |
| `$this->deleteJson()` | Simulate a JSON DELETE request |

| # | Test | Expected result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | List employees | `200 OK`, JSON data |
| 2 | Create employee | `201 Created`, employee persisted |
| 3 | Submit invalid employee data | `422 Unprocessable Entity` |
| 4 | Show an employee | `200 OK`, employee resource |
| 5 | Update an employee | Successful response and updated database record |
| 6 | Submit invalid update data | `422 Unprocessable Entity` |
| 7 | Delete an employee | Successful response and expected deletion behavior |
| 8 | Access API without authentication | `401 Unauthorized` |
| 9 | Employee attempts to view another employee | `403 Forbidden`, if the route uses the policy as expected |
| 10 | Verify the API Resource structure | Correct employee and department JSON fields |



